.TH HMAP_PRINT_TREE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
hmap_print_tree \- Print the contents of a hash map in a tree-like format

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/hmap_internal.h>

.IB int
.IB hmap_print_tree(HMAP_DB* hmap);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBhmap_print_tree\fR function prints the contents of a hash map in a tree-like format. It takes a pointer to the hash map database as an argument.

The function takes the following parameter:
.IB HMAP_DB*
A pointer to the hash map database.

The function returns an integer indicating the success or failure of the operation. Possible return values include:
.IB HMAP_SUCCESS
The hash map was successfully printed in a tree-like format.
.IB HMAP_DB_EMPTY
The hash map is empty.

The \fBhmap_print_tree\fR function is intended to be used to print the contents of a hash map in a tree-like format for debugging purposes.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBhmap_print_tree\fR function to print the contents of a hash map in a tree-like format:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/hmap_internal.h>

int main() {
    HMAP_DB* hmap_db;
    // Initialize the hash map database
    hmap_init(&hmap_db);

    // Add a tuple with a string data type
    hmap_add_tuple_with_data(&hmap_db, "example1", 8, "data1");

    // Print the contents of the hash map in a tree-like format
    int result = hmap_print_tree(hmap_db);

    if(result == HMAP_SUCCESS) {
        printf("Hash map printed successfully in a tree-like format\n");
    } else {
        printf("Failed to print hash map in a tree-like format\n");
    }

    // Free the hash map database
    hmap_destroy(&hmap_db);

    return 0;
}
.nef